const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Case Report Writing Service","serviceType":"Clinical case report writing, consent documentation and journal submission support","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/case-report/","description":"Medical case report writing for clinicians: structured to CARE, surgical cases to SCARE 2023, the consent handled before drafting.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you write a medical case report for journal publication?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Work through the CARE checklist from the outline stage rather than checking against it at the end. Thirteen items: a title containing the phrase “case report”, key words, a structured abstract, an introduction that says what this case adds, de-identified patient information, clinical findings, a dated timeline, the diagnostic assessment with its differential and its limitations, the intervention, follow-up and outcomes, a discussion, the patient’s own perspective, and a statement of informed consent. Two practical points carry more weight than their length suggests. Build the timeline first, from the record rather than from the discharge summary, because it is what exposes the gaps and because it is the item most often missing from submissions. And write the introduction last — you cannot say what the case adds until you have established what is already published, and the search that establishes it will often change your mind about the claim."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What guideline governs case reports, and which version is current?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"CARE. The current version is the 2013 statement with the explanation and elaboration paper published in the Journal of Clinical Epidemiology in 2017 — there is no later revision of the core guideline, and material claiming a “CARE 2023” is almost always describing SCARE 2023, which is the surgical guideline and a different thing. Discipline-specific CARE extensions do exist, including ones for radiology and acupuncture, and the EQUATOR Network lists them. For surgical case reports, SCARE applies alongside CARE: cite the 2023 revision in the International Journal of Surgery. A further SCARE revision appeared in 2025 in a different journal and is not yet reflected on the SCARE site or by EQUATOR, so we write to 2023 and name the journal if the 2025 version comes up. Case series are covered by PROCESS 2023 where surgical."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What consent do you need to publish a case report?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Written informed consent from the patient, for publication specifically. The ICMJE Recommendations say that identifying information should not be published in written descriptions, photographs or pedigrees unless it is essential for scientific purposes and the patient or their guardian has given written informed consent for publication; that non-essential identifying details should be omitted; and that consent should be obtained wherever there is any doubt that anonymity can be maintained. They also say plainly that masking the eye region in a photograph is inadequate protection. Three things authors get wrong. Consent to treatment is not consent to publish, and a treatment consent form does not cover it. Verbal consent is not what journals ask for, and an editor who requests the form at revision and cannot be given one will reject the paper at that point. And patients should be told that identifiable material may be accessible online after publication, which a form drafted before online publishing existed will not mention. Get it signed while the patient is in front of you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you publish a case report if the patient has died or cannot be traced?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sometimes, and the answer is the journal’s rather than ours. Practice varies by title and by jurisdiction: some journals accept consent from the next of kin, some require it, and some will consider a case with institutional ethics committee approval and every identifying detail removed. What does not vary is that a death does not remove the duty — the obligation runs to the patient’s privacy and, in many jurisdictions, continues after death. The practical route is to write to the editor before drafting, set out the position honestly, and ask what they need. Editors answer this question regularly and are usually helpful. What goes badly is submitting a manuscript and mentioning the difficulty only when the consent form is requested, because by then the editor is being asked to approve a decision that was already made."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does it cost to publish a case report?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Anything from nothing to about $1,900, for work of comparable standard — which is why the journal decision is worth more than an hour of anybody’s time. On figures read from each journal’s own page on 21 September 2026: Cureus Journals charges no article processing charge, with an editing fee of $0–300 applying only to manuscripts submitted with substantial errors, in force until 31 December 2026. Radiology Case Reports is $660. JAAD Case Reports is $850, or $390 for Grand Rounds and Dermoscopy Case of the Month. The American Journal of Case Reports is $1,100 and charges only on acceptance. The Journal of Medical Case Reports is £1,160 / $1,775 / €1,525 for case reports, with waivers available. Clinical Case Reports goes up to $1,910 with a waiver policy. BMJ Case Reports works differently again: no per-article charge, but authors publish under an annual individual or institutional fellowship, and a fellow may submit unlimited cases in the year — so check whether your hospital library already holds one, because many do and nobody tells the clinicians. Many specialty journals publish case reports with no charge at all. Charges change without notice; confirm before you submit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why do case reports get rejected?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Six reasons, in roughly the order we see them. The case is already well described, and the author searched a database once rather than properly — this is the commonest, and it is knowable before you write. The follow-up is too short, so the reviewer reads an unfinished story; for a novel treatment, anything under six months struggles. The timeline is absent or is buried in prose, so nobody can tell what followed what, which is fatal in a diagnostic-challenge or adverse-reaction case. The discussion reviews the condition instead of arguing what this case changes. Consent is missing or is the wrong kind, which surfaces at revision rather than at submission. And the case is offered to a journal that does not publish case reports at all, or has recently stopped — journals cut case report sections quietly and their author guidelines are often the last thing updated. Four of the six are decided before a single word is drafted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you write a case report for a rare condition?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Prove the diagnosis rather than stating it, and be careful about identifiability. On the first: a reviewer assessing a rare disease report is asking how you know, so genetic, histological, biochemical or imaging confirmation goes in with its method, and where no confirmatory test exists the differential has to be worked through explicitly with the relevant negatives reported. Rarity is not a finding on its own; a reader wants to know what was considered and what changed the answer. On the second: rarity and identifiability are the same property seen from two sides. A condition with a handful of reported cases, plus a region, plus an age, plus a year, identifies one person to anyone who knows them — so age goes in a band, calendar dates become intervals, and anything that is not doing clinical work comes out. Where a registry or a patient organisation exists for the condition, say so; readers with the same diagnosis find these papers."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"The case involves a drug reaction. Is publishing it enough?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and the two duties are separate. A suspected adverse drug reaction should be reported to your national pharmacovigilance scheme whether or not it is ever published — the MHRA Yellow Card scheme in the UK, FDA MedWatch in the United States, your national competent authority feeding EudraVigilance across the EU, and the Pharmacovigilance Programme of India through the CDSCO. That report takes minutes, reaches a signal-detection system within weeks, and is what actually changes a label; a journal article may take a year and reaches a different audience for a different purpose. Journals increasingly ask on submission whether the reaction was reported, so doing it first is also practically convenient. For the manuscript itself, the work is causality: temporality stated precisely, the dechallenge and any rechallenge documented with dates, alternatives excluded and the exclusions shown, and a published causality instrument applied with its workings reported rather than its score alone."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I presented this case at a meeting already. Is that a problem?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Almost never a problem, and quite often a problem if you do not mention it. Presenting a case at a regional meeting, publishing the abstract in a supplement and then submitting the full report to a journal is entirely normal practice. What journals object to is undisclosed overlap: the same patient appearing in two places with neither citing the other, which is redundant publication and which COPE has a flowchart for. The remedy is trivial and entirely one-sided. Say so in the cover letter, cite the abstract if it was published, and let the editor decide. Almost every editor says yes. The ones who would have said no would have found out anyway, usually from a reviewer who was at the meeting."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"If Pubrica writes it, does that have to be declared?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and the declaration is exactly what makes it legitimate. The ICMJE lists writing assistance, technical editing, language editing and proofreading among the activities that do not on their own qualify somebody for authorship, says such contributors should be acknowledged with their specific contribution described, and advises editors to require that the corresponding author obtain written permission from everybody acknowledged. It also requires disclosure of any sponsor’s role in the writing of the report. So a Pubrica writer is named in the acknowledgements, we supply the wording and the written permission, and you keep authorship. Where a contribution does meet all four criteria — substantial contribution to conception or to acquisition, analysis or interpretation; drafting or critical revision; final approval; and accountability — we say so and let you decide; nothing bars a writer from being an author, it is the contribution that decides. Declared assistance is the opposite of ghostwriting rather than a polite version of it. The same principle governs AI: under the ICMJE’s January 2026 update a chatbot cannot be an author, AI used for writing assistance is declared in the acknowledgements, and AI used to generate or analyse anything is described in the methods with the tool, version and prompts."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Physician writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Case report","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/case-report/"}]}];

